    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1003">

                <pb facs="1129" n="1119" sameAs="frus:frus1944v04/pg1119"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">861.24/8–2144: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the Soviet Union</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Harriman</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Moscow</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1944-08-21T19:00:00+03:00">August 21, 1944—7
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received 8:39 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>3097. The Military Mission has received an answer from the Red Air Staff
                    regarding our offer to transport by air ball bearings from Sweden. It is stated
                    that they have no instructions on this question and therefore inquiry should be
                    made through me to the Foreign Office. Letter ends by suggesting possibility
                    that the Soviet Civil Aviation will be given the task of carrying out this
                    mission.</p>
                <p>I am not taking this matter up again with the Foreign Office until I receive a
                    reply from the Department to my No. 2938, August 11, 10 a.m. [<hi
                        rendition="#italic">p.m.</hi>] One cannot help but have the impression that
                    the Soviets do not urgently need these bearings.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Harriman</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
